window.EN = {
  "meta.title": "How to reset your dopamine system",
  "top.home": "Renato Shirakashi",
  "top.kicker": "Dopamine · 2026",
  "top.sound": "♪ listen",
  "hero.eyebrow": "Interactive essay · November 2026",
  "hero.h1": "\n    <span class=\"mark\">How to reset your</span>\n    <span class=\"mark l2\">dopamine system</span>\n  ",
  "hero.sub": "Dopamine makes the body move, marks what surprises us and teaches the brain to want again. Let’s dive into this magic molecule and understand the hype behind the detox.",
  "hero.byline": "by Renato Shirakashi",
  "s00.h": "To reset dopamine, you first need to know what it does",
  "s00.step": "00 · The question",
  "s00.p1": "On November 10, 2026, the Huberman Lab channel posted an eleven-minute clip titled “How to Reset Your Dopamine System &amp; Overcome Addiction”. In it, the Stanford psychiatrist Anna Lembke explains addiction with the image of a balance in the brain and proposes thirty days without the drug of choice. The title assumes three things: that there is a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dopamina\">dopamine</span> system, that this system has a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"setpoint\">set point</span>, and that it can be returned to where it started.",
  "s00.p2": "The word reached the title of a neuroscience video after five years of growth outside the laboratory. On Google, worldwide searches for “dopamine” more than tripled between 2018 and 2026. “Dopamine detox” rose from almost zero in 2019 to a peak in November 2024. In Brazil, almost nobody searches for the protocol, and searches for “dopamina” peaked in June 2023, a year after the Brazilian edition of Lembke’s book, <em>Nação Dopamina</em> (<em>Dopamine Nation</em>).",
  "s00.p3": "To know what a reset could change, you need to know what dopamine does. Before we begin, mark the functions you think it has:",
  "w00.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What does dopamine do?</b><span class=\"wdo\">Tap the cards you think are right, then save. Your answer comes back corrected at the end.</span>",
  "w00.cap": "Your answer is saved in this browser and comes back, corrected, at the end of the essay.",
  "s01.h": "Dopamine was discovered as the molecule that makes the body move",
  "s01.step": "01 · The movement molecule",
  "s01.p1": "Dopamine was discovered as the molecule that makes the body move. Until the mid-1950s, pharmacologists treated it as a step in the making of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"noradrenalina\">noradrenaline</span>, with no function of its own. In 1957, the Swedish pharmacologist Arvid Carlsson gave <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"reserpina\">reserpine</span> to rabbits and mice. Reserpine, then used against high blood pressure and <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"psicose\">psychosis</span>, empties the brain’s stores of several <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"neurotransmissor\">signaling molecules</span>. The animals went still, their eyelids drooping. Carlsson injected <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ldopa\">L-DOPA</span>, the raw material of dopamine, and within minutes the rabbits were walking again. The following year he showed that the recovery tracked the return of dopamine to the brain, while noradrenaline stayed depleted. The three frames below show the experiment:",
  "w01a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Without dopamine, the rabbit cannot move</b><span class=\"wdo\">The three frames show the rabbit before, 16 hours after reserpine and about 15 minutes after L-DOPA. Compare the dopamine meter in each one.</span>",
  "w01a.cap": "Carlsson, Lindqvist and Magnusson (<i>Nature</i>, 1957); Carlsson et al. (<i>Science</i>, 1958). The times of 16 hours and about 15 minutes come from the paper and from historical accounts.",
  "s01.p2": "The link to a human disease came in 1960. The Austrian pharmacologist Oleh Hornykiewicz measured dopamine in the brains of six people who had died with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"parkinson\">Parkinson’s disease</span> and found, in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"estriado\">striatum</span>, the region that initiates movement, about a tenth of the normal amount. In 1961, he and the neurologist Walther Birkmayer injected L-DOPA into twenty patients, and their slowness of movement vanished for a few hours. In 1967, George Cotzias showed that high oral doses, raised slowly, gave a lasting effect. Levodopa was approved in the United States in 1970 and remains the first-line treatment for Parkinson’s. In 2000, Carlsson shared the Nobel Prize in Medicine with Paul Greengard and Eric Kandel. Hornykiewicz was left out, and about 230 scientists signed a letter of protest.",
  "s01.p3": "L-DOPA works because the brain makes dopamine on a two-step assembly line. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tirosina\">Tyrosine</span>, an amino acid from food, becomes L-DOPA through an <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"enzima\">enzyme</span> called <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"th\">tyrosine hydroxylase</span>, and L-DOPA becomes dopamine through another enzyme, fast and loosely regulated. The first step sets the pace of the whole line, and dopamine itself brakes that enzyme when there is a surplus. That is why eating more tyrosine barely changes production, and giving L-DOPA skips the bottleneck. Dopamine in the blood cannot cross the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"barreira\">barrier that protects the brain</span>, and L-DOPA can. The model below compares the two:",
  "w01b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Where dopamine production bottlenecks</b><span class=\"wdo\">In the first panel, compare the normal diet, four times the tyrosine and L-DOPA. In the second, the path of 100 levodopa molecules without and with carbidopa.</span>",
  "w01b.cap": "Tyrosine hydroxylase limits production, so extra tyrosine barely changes the output. Levodopa is now given with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"carbidopa\">carbidopa</span>, which blocks the conversion outside the brain and allows a dose about four to five times smaller.",
  "s02.h": "Few neurons make dopamine, and each one reaches a huge part of the brain",
  "s02.step": "02 · Half a million",
  "s02.p1": "The human brain has about 86 billion <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"neuronio\">neurons</span>. The ones that make dopamine and release it into the rest of the brain number about half a million, gathered in two small nuclei in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mesencefalo\">midbrain</span>, the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"sn\">substantia nigra</span> and the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"atv\">ventral tegmental area</span>. That is one dopamine neuron for every 170,000. The field below has 170,000 dots. Zoom in until you find the amber dot, then tap it:",
  "w02a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">1 dopamine neuron for every 170,000</b><span class=\"wdo\">The amber dot is one dopamine neuron. Zoom in to see the others, or tap show and then the dot.</span>",
  "w02a.cap": "About 86 billion neurons (Azevedo et al., 2009) and about half a million dopamine neurons in the human midbrain (Pakkenberg et al., 1991; estimates range from 400,000 to 600,000).",
  "s02.p2": "Each of these neurons reaches very far. In 2009, Wakoto Matsuda and colleagues reconstructed eight rat dopamine neurons one by one, following every branch. The <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"axonio\">axon</span> of a single neuron added up to an average of 46.7 centimeters inside the striatum, in a brain about two centimeters long, and covered on average 2.7% of the region’s volume. In 2012, Paul Bolam and Eleftheria Pissadaki estimated what this would mean in a human brain: <span class=\"mark\">about 4.5 meters of axon and 1 million to 2.4 million <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"sinapse\">synapses</span> per neuron</span>. A typical striatal neuron makes 300 to 500 synapses. Unroll the axon of a human neuron and compare it with the things beside it:",
  "w02b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The size of a single dopamine neuron</b><span class=\"wdo\">Drag the slider to unroll the axon and compare it with a person, a table and a car.</span>",
  "w02b.cap": "Matsuda et al. (<i>J Neurosci</i>, 2009), eight rat neurons. Bolam and Pissadaki (<i>Mov Disord</i>, 2012), a human estimate that assumes the same density of innervation as in the rat. The human number has never been measured.",
  "s02.p3": "Bolam and Pissadaki propose that this size has a cost. Maintaining meters of axon and millions of synapses takes a great deal of energy, which would help explain why these neurons are among the first to die in Parkinson’s.",
  "s02.p4": "Much of the dopamine also spreads widely, reaching many targets at once. Most dopamine terminals do not form a closed synapse with a neighboring neuron. The dopamine leaves, diffuses through the space between cells and reaches <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"receptor\">receptors</span> a few micrometers away, like a watering can over a flower bed. The four main routes it travels are drawn below as subway lines. The cards under the map say what each one does and which drug acts on it:",
  "w02c.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Dopamine's four pathways in the brain</b><span class=\"wdo\">Each line carries dopamine to one region. The cards say what it does, what happens when it fails and which drug acts on it. Below, one terminal seen up close.</span>",
  "w02c.cap": "Björklund and Dunnett (<i>Trends Neurosci</i>, 2007). The drawing is schematic; the four pathways arise from different groups of neurons and have different functions.",
  "s03.h": "The dopamine neuron keeps a background rhythm and sends fast signals on top of it",
  "s03.step": "03 · Two rhythms",
  "s03.p1": "A dopamine neuron at rest fires on its own, at a regular pace, about four to five times per second. Anthony Grace and Benjamin Bunney described this rhythm in 1984 and showed that it continues even in an isolated slice of brain. Sometimes the neuron breaks rhythm and lets out a burst of two to ten spikes at 15 or 30 per second, lasting one to three tenths of a second. Other times it falls silent for the same length of time. The regular rhythm keeps up a background level of dopamine, and the bursts and silences send fast signals on top of that background.",
  "s03.p2": "Receptors read the two signals differently. There are five types of dopamine receptor, grouped in two families. Receptors of the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"d2\">D2 family</span> are very sensitive and are already partly occupied at the background level. Receptors of the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"d1\">D1 family</span> are less sensitive and respond mainly to bursts. Turn on the sound, listen to the background rhythm and tap the two buttons:",
  "w03.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The two rhythms of a dopamine neuron</b><span class=\"wdo\">The trace already shows the background rhythm, one burst and one pause. Turn on the sound and tap surprise or letdown to trigger your own.</span>",
  "w03.cap": "Grace and Bunney (<i>J Neurosci</i>, 1984). The occupancy curves are schematic and show only the difference in sensitivity between the two receptor families.",
  "s03.p3": "The neuron in the page margin has been playing this same rhythm since the start of the essay. At some moments it fires bursts and pauses, and the reason appears in section 04.",
  "s04.h": "The dopamine numbers that circulate come from rats and depend on the instrument",
  "s04.step": "04 · How it is measured",
  "s04.p1": "Almost all the dopamine numbers in circulation come from experiments on rats and from a technique called microdialysis. Researchers implant a thin probe in the brain and collect, every ten or twenty minutes, a little of the fluid that bathes the neurons. The result comes out as a percentage of the animal’s own resting level, its baseline. In the Huberman Lab video, Lembke cites four of these numbers: chocolate raises dopamine 55% above baseline, sex 100%, nicotine 150% and methamphetamine 1,000%. Before you see the bars, drag each one to the height you think is right:",
  "w04a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much does each thing raise dopamine in a rat?</b><span class=\"wdo\">Drag each bar to your guess, then tap reveal.</span>",
  "w04a.cap": "Extracellular dopamine in the nucleus accumbens of rats, in % above baseline, according to Lembke. One thousand percent above baseline means eleven times the resting level.",
  "s04.p2": "The four numbers come from two slides in a teaching package from the US National Institute on Drug Abuse, made in the late 1990s, that combines three rat studies (Di Chiara and Imperato, 1988; Fiorino and Phillips, 1997; Bassareo and Di Chiara, 1999). In the original graphs, food reaches about 50% above baseline, sex 95% and cocaine about 230%. The 1,000% peak belongs to amphetamine, a cousin of methamphetamine, and was measured as a percentage of baseline, which comes to ten times the resting level, or 900% above it. Nicotine reaches about 120% above. The food in the experiment was an Italian corn-and-cheese snack called Fonzies.",
  "s04.p3": "Each microdialysis sample collapses ten to twenty minutes of dopamine into a single number. The burst from section 03 lasts about two tenths of a second, and six thousand of them would fit in a twenty-minute sample. Since the 1980s, other techniques have shortened this window. Fast-scan voltammetry, with a carbon fiber thinner than a hair, measures every tenth of a second. Fluorescent sensors created in 2018 make dopamine glow inside the brains of mice within tens of milliseconds. In living people, almost everything we know comes from positron emission tomography (PET), which takes an hour to an hour and a half to produce one number and measures dopamine indirectly. In the graph below, the top curve is a dopamine signal with bursts. Choose a technique and see what it records of that signal:",
  "w04b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What each instrument can see of dopamine</b><span class=\"wdo\">Pick a technique and compare the real signal with what it records.</span>",
  "w04b.cap": "Kishida et al. (<i>PLoS One</i>, 2011; <i>PNAS</i>, 2016); Sun et al. (<i>Cell</i>, 2018); Patriarchi et al. (<i>Science</i>, 2018). The signal is simulated; each technique’s window is the typical one in the literature.",
  "s05.h": "Dopamine marks surprise, the gap between what was expected and what came",
  "s05.step": "04 · The surprise signal",
  "s05.p1": "Wolfram Schultz recorded dopamine neurons in monkeys in the 1980s and 1990s. The monkey saw a light and, one second later, received a drop of juice. At first, the neurons fired a burst when the juice arrived. After many repetitions, they began to fire when the light came on and to stay at the background rhythm when the juice arrived. When the expected juice did not come, they fell silent for an instant. In 1997, Schultz, Peter Dayan and Read Montague described this signal in <em>Science</em> as a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"erro\">prediction error</span>, the difference between what the animal expected and what it received. In the model below, each row is one trial, and the neuron has already been through 41 of them:",
  "w05a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The neuron learns where the surprise is</b><span class=\"wdo\">Each row is one trial, from the first at the bottom to the latest at the top. The burst moves from the juice to the light. The buttons keep training it.</span>",
  "w05a.cap": "Model of the signal described by Schultz, Dayan and Montague (<i>Science</i>, 1997). The ramp at a 50% chance comes from Fiorillo, Tobler and Schultz (<i>Science</i>, 2003). The learning speed is illustrative.",
  "s05.p2": "Notice that <span class=\"mark\">the neuron stops reacting to the juice once the juice becomes predictable.</span> The dopamine signal marks surprise. The neuron in the page margin does the same with your answers. It fires a burst when a result contradicts your guess and falls silent when an expected result fails to appear.",
  "s05.p3": "The idea of learning from surprise was born in psychology and passed through computer science before reaching the neuron. In 1972, Robert Rescorla and Allan Wagner proposed that an animal learns in proportion to the difference between what it expected and what happened. In 1988, Richard Sutton turned the idea into a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"td\">temporal-difference learning</span> algorithm, with which a machine learns on its own to predict future rewards. Schultz’s data showed that dopamine neurons compute something very close to this algorithm’s error. The same family of algorithms helped DeepMind’s AlphaGo program beat the champion Lee Sedol in 2016. In March 2025, Sutton and Andrew Barto received the Turing Award for the foundations of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"rl\">reinforcement learning</span>.",
  "s05.p4": "The exchange then ran in the opposite direction. In 2017, DeepMind researchers showed that artificial intelligence agents learn better when they predict the entire distribution of possible rewards in addition to its average. In 2020, Will Dabney and colleagues looked for this in mouse dopamine neurons and found it: each neuron has its own reference point, some optimistic and others pessimistic, and together they encode the distribution. Below, the same choir has learned two lotteries with the same mean:",
  "w05b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Optimist and pessimist neurons</b><span class=\"wdo\">Two lotteries with the same mean. Compare where each neuron settles and the shape the group rebuilds.</span>",
  "w05b.cap": "Dabney et al. (<i>Nature</i>, 2020); Lowet et al. (<i>Nature</i>, 2025), who found striatal neurons with D1 receptors reading the better-than-expected side and neurons with D2 reading the worse side. Dabney’s data come from few animals.",
  "s06.h": "Dopamine drives wanting and effort, and weighs little on liking",
  "s06.step": "05 · Wanting",
  "s06.p1": "If dopamine marks surprise and teaches what is worth repeating, the remaining question is whether it also produces pleasure. In 1998, Kent Berridge and Terry Robinson destroyed up to 99% of the dopamine in rats with a toxin. The rats kept making the same face of satisfaction at sugar water as normal rats, and the same face of disgust at <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"quinino\">quinine</span>. They stopped going after the food. Berridge called the urge to go after something wanting, which depends on dopamine, and the reaction of satisfaction liking, which depends on <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"opioides\">opioids</span> acting in a spot of about one cubic millimeter in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"accumbens\">nucleus accumbens</span>.",
  "s06.p2": "John Salamone measured wanting as effort. In 1991, he gave hungry rats a choice: press a lever five times for a sweet pellet, or eat the ordinary chow lying free on the cage floor. Normal rats worked for the pellet. With a low dose of a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"bloqueador\">dopamine blocker</span>, they switched to the chow and ate the same total amount. Their appetite and their taste for sweets were still there. What dropped was their willingness to pay for the sweet with effort. In the model below, you are the rat. Lower the dopamine and try to keep pressing the lever:",
  "w06a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">With less dopamine, effort weighs more</b><span class=\"wdo\">Hold the lever to earn the sweet pellet. Then lower the dopamine and feel the effort grow.</span>",
  "w06a.cap": "Salamone et al. (<i>Psychopharmacology</i>, 1991); Salamone and Correa (<i>Neuron</i>, 2012). In humans, Treadway et al. (2012) found more dopamine release in the striatum of people who accepted harder tasks for money. The weight of the lever is illustrative.",
  "s06.p3": "<span class=\"mark\">Studies in humans show the same pattern.</span> In 2001, Brian Knutson used <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"rm\">magnetic resonance imaging</span> to show that the nucleus accumbens activates when a person expects to win money, and barely at all when the money arrives. In 2009, Tali Sharot gave levodopa to volunteers while they imagined vacations in different places. Later, they rated the vacations imagined under levodopa as more pleasurable. Dopamine changed the forecast of pleasure. In 2002, Troels Kjaer used <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pet\">PET</span> to measure a rise in dopamine during a guided meditation session, and the participants reported less desire to act, with pleasure and relaxation equal to those of the control condition. In 2017, a PET study showed that half an hour of laughing with friends releases the body’s own opioids. The human data have one known exception: in 2019, Laura Ferreri gave levodopa to 27 people, and they reported more pleasure while listening to music.",
  "s06.p4": "The essay <a href=\"como-querer-menos.html\">How to want less</a> deals with wanting and liking in everyday life. For this page, it is enough to keep in mind that dopamine weighs more on wanting, effort and expectation than on the satisfaction of receiving.",
  "s07.h": "More dopamine helps up to a point and then gets in the way",
  "s07.step": "06 · More is not always better",
  "s07.p1": "Dopamine in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pfc\">prefrontal cortex</span>, the front part of the brain that holds information for a few seconds and controls attention, follows an <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ui\">inverted-U curve</span>. With too little, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mt\">working memory</span> fails. With too much, it also fails. Roshan Cools and Mark D’Esposito reviewed the studies in 2011. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"bromo\">Bromocriptine</span>, which mimics dopamine, <span class=\"mark\">improved working memory in people with low capacity and worsened it in people with high capacity</span>. In 2020, a study gave 2 grams of tyrosine, the raw material of dopamine, to 45 young adults. The group average did not change, because those who did poorly improved and those who did well got worse. See what the same dose does to three people at different points on the curve:",
  "w07a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The same drug helps some and hurts others</b><span class=\"wdo\">All three get the same dose. Whoever was below the peak rises, and whoever was at or past the peak falls.</span>",
  "w07a.cap": "Cools and D’Esposito (<i>Biol Psychiatry</i>, 2011); Jongkees et al. (2020); Farrell et al. (2012). The curve is schematic. Individual genetic effects are small and poorly replicated in large samples.",
  "s07.p2": "The same logic of a narrow window appears in the drugs that block dopamine. In 1976, Philip Seeman’s group, in <em>Nature</em>, and Solomon Snyder’s, in <em>Science</em>, compared the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"antipsic\">antipsychotics</span> in clinical use. The daily dose of each one tracked how tightly it bound to the receptor now called D2. The relationship spanned three <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ordem\">orders of magnitude</span>. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"afinidade\">Affinity</span> for other receptors did not follow the same pattern:",
  "w07b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Each antipsychotic's dose follows the D2 receptor</b><span class=\"wdo\">The same eight drugs against two receptors. With D2, the points follow a line. With the serotonin receptor, they scatter into a cloud.</span>",
  "w07b.cap": "Seeman et al. (<i>Nature</i>, 1976); Creese, Burt and Snyder (<i>Science</i>, 1976). The dose and affinity values are approximate, taken from Seeman (2002), and serve to show the shape of the relationship.",
  "s07.p3": "In 2000, Shitij Kapur used PET to measure how many D2 receptors <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"haloperidol\">haloperidol</span> occupied in 22 patients in their first episode of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"psicose\">psychosis</span>. Clinical improvement became more likely above 65% <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ocupacao\">occupancy</span>. Above 72%, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"prolactina\">prolactin</span> rose, because dopamine is the brake the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hipotalamo\">hypothalamus</span> uses to hold back this hormone, and releasing the brake can make the body produce milk outside pregnancy. Above 78%, rigidity and tremor appeared, because the same molecule controls movement. Dopamine also acts in a region of the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tronco\">brainstem</span> that sits outside the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"barreira\">brain’s barrier</span> and samples the blood for toxins. <span class=\"mark\">That is why drugs that block dopamine work against nausea.</span> See what each band of occupancy causes:",
  "w07c.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The narrow window of antipsychotics</b><span class=\"wdo\">What each band of D2 receptor occupancy turns on in the body. The useful band runs from 65% to 78%.</span>",
  "w07c.cap": "Kapur et al. (<i>Am J Psychiatry</i>, 2000), 22 patients on 1 to 2.5 mg of haloperidol a day. The most consistent abnormality in <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"esquizo\">schizophrenia</span> lies in dopamine production, which is higher in the striatum of many patients (Howes and Kapur, 2009).",
  "s08.h": "Drugs that mimic dopamine changed what Parkinson's patients wanted",
  "s08.step": "07 · An accidental experiment",
  "s08.p1": "Parkinson’s drugs show, in millions of people, what dopamine does to wanting. Besides levodopa, many patients take <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"agonista\">dopamine agonists</span>, such as <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"prami\">pramipexole</span> and <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ropini\">ropinirole</span>, which mimic the molecule and favor D2 and D3 receptors, which are very dense in the nucleus accumbens. In the early 2000s, neurologists began to describe patients with no history of addiction who <span class=\"mark\">started gambling compulsively, shopping without control and having new sexual urges after starting the drug</span>.",
  "s08.p2": "In 2010, Daniel Weintraub published the DOMINION study, with 3,090 Parkinson’s patients at 46 centers in the United States and Canada. Among those taking an agonist, 17.1% had some <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tci\">impulse control disorder</span>. Among those not taking one, 6.9%. In 2018, Jean-Christophe Corvol followed 411 French patients for five years. Among those who had ever used an agonist, 51.5% developed such a disorder. Among those who never had, 12.4%. The risk rose with the dose, and the disorders receded when the drug was stopped:",
  "w08.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Parkinson's drugs and wanting</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare people on and off agonists. The risk rises with the dose and recedes when the drug stops.</span>",
  "w08.cap": "Weintraub et al. (<i>Arch Neurol</i>, 2010), a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"transversal\">cross-sectional study</span> funded by Boehringer Ingelheim, the maker of pramipexole. Corvol et al. (<i>Neurology</i>, 2018), the DIGPD <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"coorte\">cohort</span>. The shape of the fall after stopping the drug is illustrative.",
  "s08.p3": "In France, Didier Jambart, a father and town councilor, took ropinirole from 2003. He began gambling until he lost the family’s savings, developed a sexual compulsion he had not had before, and stole to gamble. The symptoms vanished when he stopped the drug, in 2005. In 2011, a court in Nantes ordered the manufacturer, GSK, to pay 117,000 euros, and on appeal, in 2012, the amount rose to about 197,000 euros. Since the mid-2000s, <span class=\"mark\">the labels of these drugs have warned about gambling and hypersexuality</span>. In 2016, the US <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"fda\">Food and Drug Administration</span> issued the same warning for <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"aripi\">aripiprazole</span>, an antipsychotic that partially stimulates the D2 receptor.",
  "s08.p4": "Parkinson’s also shows how much the system can withstand before it fails. Motor symptoms appear only when about 80% of the dopamine in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"putamen\">putamen</span> is already gone. Four years after diagnosis, the dopamine markers in this region are almost absent (Kordower, 2013). The number of people with Parkinson’s worldwide is expected to go from 11.9 million in 2021 to 25.2 million in 2050, almost entirely because of population aging. In April 2025, two studies in <em>Nature</em> reported transplants of dopamine-producing cells made from <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"celtronco\">stem cells</span>, which were safe and showed signs that the cells survived in the brain.",
  "s09.h": "Everyday pleasures barely move dopamine; age, sleep and drugs move it more",
  "s09.step": "08 · What moves your dopamine",
  "s09.p1": "In people, the most widely used measure is <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"pet\">PET</span> with a tracer called <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"raclopride\">raclopride</span>, which competes with dopamine for D2 receptors. When there is more dopamine near the receptors, less tracer binds, and the signal drops. A 10% drop in the signal shows that something released dopamine. It does not show that dopamine rose by 10%. The scan itself varies by about 4% when the same person is measured twice, weeks apart, with nothing different. Before you see the graph, rank these from largest to smallest effect: music that gives you chills, video games, coffee, cigarettes and <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"metilfen\">methylphenidate</span>:",
  "w09a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Everyday pleasures and drugs on one ruler</b><span class=\"wdo\">Put the five in order from largest to smallest effect, then tap reveal.</span>",
  "w09a.cap": "Alakurtti et al. (2015, noise); Volkow et al. (2015, caffeine); Kjaer et al. (2002, meditation); Salimpoor et al. (2011, music); Zald et al. (2004, money); Koepp et al. (1998, video games, critically reexamined by Egerton et al., 2009); Martinez et al. (2003, amphetamine); de la Fuente-Fernández et al. (2001, placebo); Volkow et al. (2001, methylphenidate); Brody et al. (2004, cigarettes). Samples of 7 to 20 people.",
  "s09.p2": "Notice that music, meditation and money sit one or two <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ruido\">noise widths</span> from zero, and that the drugs and cigarettes sit two to four times farther out. Coffee pushes the signal the other way. Nora Volkow measured this in 2015 in 20 people who took 300 milligrams of caffeine, the equivalent of about three cups of brewed coffee. Caffeine increased the availability of receptors, which indicates that it acts on the receptor itself and leaves the amount of dopamine released unchanged. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"placebo\">Placebo</span> in Parkinson’s patients released dopamine comparable to that of a drug, through the expectation of improvement alone.",
  "s09.p3": "Sleep loss shows how the same measurement can be read two ways. In 2008, Volkow’s group saw the raclopride signal drop after a night without sleep and interpreted the drop as more dopamine. In 2012, the same group repeated the experiment with an extra test and concluded that the dopamine released was the same, and that what dropped was the number of available receptors. Exercise has two acute PET studies with opposite results: thirty minutes on a treadmill changed nothing in 12 people (Wang, 2000), and recumbent cycling showed release in 16 people (Ando, 2024). Two studies on sunlight and season used different measures and pointed in opposite directions.",
  "s09.p4": "What reliably changes the receptors is less glamorous. In 2017, Teresa Karrer pooled 95 studies with 2,611 healthy adults and measured how much each part of the system loses per decade of life. Here is each part at 20, 40, 60 and 80:",
  "w09b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What age does to the dopamine system</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare each part of the system at 20, 40, 60 and 80.</span>",
  "w09b.cap": "Karrer et al. (<i>Neurobiol Aging</i>, 2017), 95 PET and <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"spect\">SPECT</span> studies. The studies are cross-sectional, so differences between generations are mixed in with aging.",
  "s09.p5": "What is sold to boost dopamine almost always fails to reach the brain. Tyrosine fills the entrance of the assembly line from section 01 and barely changes the output. The 250% increase attributed to cold baths comes from a 2000 study in which ten men sat immersed up to the neck in water at 14 °C for an hour, and the measurement was taken in the blood. About half of the body’s dopamine is made in the gut, and it stays outside the brain’s barrier. No blood or urine test measures the brain’s dopamine. The exception is <em><span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mucuna\">Mucuna pruriens</span></em>, a legume sold as a supplement, whose seeds contain real levodopa:",
  "w09c.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much levodopa a Mucuna supplement contains</b><span class=\"wdo\">Each capsule is one product, filled to the levodopa measured. Compare it with the range of a Parkinson’s tablet.</span>",
  "w09c.cap": "Cohen et al. (<i>JAMA Neurology</i>, 2022), Mucuna supplements bought in the United States. Levodopa combined with nonselective <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"imao\">MAO inhibitor</span> antidepressants can cause a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"crise\">hypertensive crisis</span>, and the same rule applies to Mucuna.",
  "s10.h": "Repeated doses make the brain compensate, and the resting point sinks",
  "s10.step": "09 · The balance",
  "s10.p1": "The Huberman Lab video deals with an extreme case of the system: what happens when dopamine is pushed up many times in a row. Lembke explains it with a balance in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"circuito\">reward circuit</span>, with pleasure on one side and pain on the other. When she watches a gossip video at night, the balance tips toward pleasure, and the brain puts weights on the pain side until it levels out. Lembke calls these weights gremlins. They come off only after tipping the other side by the same amount, and that interval is the hangover, or the urge to watch one more video.",
  "s10.p2": "The image is an illustrated version of a 1974 theory. The psychologists Richard Solomon and John Corbit proposed that every stimulus that affects mood triggers a fast process and recruits an opposing, slower process. With repetition, the slow process grows. In the model below, each tap on <code>one dose</code> puts a weight on the pleasure side. Give one dose, advance the clock, and then shorten the interval between doses:",
  "w10a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The pleasure and pain balance</b><span class=\"wdo\">Tap one dose and advance the clock. Then shorten the interval and repeat for 30 days.</span>",
  "w10a.cap": "The balance is Lembke’s metaphor (<i>Dopamine Nation</i>, 2021) for the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"oponente\">opponent-process theory</span> (Solomon and Corbit, 1974) and for <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"alostase\">allostasis</span> (Koob and Le Moal, 2001). The shape of the curves follows the theory; the values are illustrative.",
  "s10.p3": "Notice that below a certain interval, the dashed line goes down and does not come back on its own while the doses continue. George Koob and Michel Le Moal called this shift allostasis. In rats that self-administer <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cocaina\">cocaine</span> at will, the electrical stimulation needed to activate the reward circuit increases, and it increases more in those that took more (Markou and Koob, 1991).",
  "s10.p4": "Drugs push the system further and for longer than natural rewards. In rats, a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"microdialise\">thin probe</span> in the nucleus accumbens collects the fluid that bathes the neurons every ten or twenty minutes. With it, food raises dopamine to about one and a half times the resting level and returns to normal in less than an hour and a half. <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"anfetamina\">Amphetamine</span> takes it to ten times the resting level and keeps it high for more than five hours. This experiment is the source of the “1,000%” that Lembke attributes to <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"metanfetamina\">methamphetamine</span> in the video:",
  "w10b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How much and for how long dopamine rises in a rat</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare how high and how long each curve stays up, and the area above baseline below.</span>",
  "w10b.cap": "Di Chiara and Imperato (<i>PNAS</i>, 1988); Fiorino et al. (<i>J Neurosci</i>, 1997); Bassareo and Di Chiara (1999). Curves read by hand from the NIDA slides, with a margin of 5% to 10%. The doses were chosen by the researchers of each study. Morphine is shown at 10 mg/kg; the other doses are in the table.",
  "s10.p5": "Speed matters too. In 2000, Volkow gave cocaine to 32 users by three routes, and the blockade of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dat\">dopamine transporters</span> was similar for all three. The peak high came at 1.4 minutes with smoked cocaine, 3.1 minutes with injected cocaine and 14.6 minutes with snorted cocaine. Oral methylphenidate occupies the same transporter, takes an hour to peak in the brain and, at clinical doses, rarely produces a high.",
  "s10.p6": "In people with addiction, PET shows a turned-down system. Methamphetamine users had 16% fewer available D2 receptors in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"caudado\">caudate</span> than volunteers without addiction (Volkow, 2001), a drop equivalent to about two decades of aging on the graph in section 08. In 2009, Diana Martinez used a drug to deplete volunteers’ dopamine for a day and measured how many receptors became free. In healthy people, resting dopamine occupied about 11% of D2 receptors. In people dependent on cocaine, about 6%. This is the closest measure of a baseline dopamine level that exists in humans, and it requires a pharmacological protocol at a PET center. Part of the difference may exist before the drug: in 2006, Michael Nader measured the receptors of twelve monkeys before any contact with cocaine, and those with fewer receptors were the ones that later self-administered the most.",
  "s11.h": "Thirty days improve mood, and habits and receptors take months",
  "s11.step": "10 · Thirty days",
  "s11.p1": "The video’s prescription is thirty days without the drug of choice. Lembke says that the first 10 to 14 days are the worst and that, in weeks three and four, about 80% of her patients feel much better. The 80% figure comes from her clinical experience. The closest published data come from a 1988 study of 191 men hospitalized for alcoholism: on admission, 42% had clinically significant depression, and by the fourth week, 6%, with the largest drop in the second week. First, draw how you think a person feels over the thirty days after quitting:",
  "w11a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How a person feels in the 30 days after quitting</b><span class=\"wdo\">Draw your curve on the chart, then tap show the data.</span>",
  "w11a.cap": "Brown and Schuckit (<i>J Stud Alcohol</i>, 1988); Hughes (2007); Budney et al. (2003); Heilig et al. (2010). The paper reports the entry and week-4 values and the largest drop in week 2; the bars for weeks 1 to 3 are estimates. The dashed curve is the clinical pattern Lembke describes, with no published measurement.",
  "s11.p2": "Experiments with screens show a similar curve in behaviors that no manual classifies as addiction. In 2025, Johannes Thrul reanalyzed the studies of social media breaks. <span class=\"mark\">Breaks shorter than a week worsened mood, breaks of a week or more improved it, and the best result appeared near three weeks.</span> The same year, Noah Castelo blocked mobile internet on the phones of 467 American adults for two weeks. Screen time fell from 314 to 161 minutes a day, and mental health improved with an <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"efeito\">effect size</span> of 0.56 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dp\">standard deviations</span>, even counting the three quarters who did not keep the block until the end:",
  "w11b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How long a screen break needs to be</b><span class=\"wdo\">See where the curve crosses zero and where the best result sits, near three weeks.</span>",
  "w11b.cap": "Thrul et al. (2025), a reanalysis of 19 small studies; Castelo et al. (<i>PNAS Nexus</i>, 2025), with no placebo group; Allcott et al. (2020); NBER w33697 (2025).",
  "s11.p3": "Studies that measure the brain give longer timelines. In people dependent on cocaine, D2 receptors remained low three to four months after detoxification. In alcoholics, there was no measurable recovery between the first and fourth months. In five methamphetamine users, dopamine transporters rose about 19% after 12 to 17 months without the drug. In a 2025 interview with CNN, Lembke said that the thirty days reset the reward pathways “at least <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"fenomeno\">phenomenologically</span>”, that is, in what the person feels. Habits also take longer. In 2024, a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"meta\">meta-analysis</span> of 20 studies with 2,601 people found <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mediana\">medians</span> of 59 to 66 days for a health habit to become automatic, with individual variation from 4 to 335 days:",
  "w11c.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Mood, habit and receptors run on different clocks</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the three timelines with the 30-day band on the left.</span>",
  "w11c.cap": "Brown and Schuckit (1988); Singh et al. (<i>Healthcare</i>, 2024); Lally et al. (2010); Volkow et al. (1993, 2001, 2002). The PET studies have 5 to 20 people.",
  "s11.p4": "Most people get out. In a sample of 43,093 American adults, the lifetime probability of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"remissao\">remission</span> for those who had ever been dependent was 83.7% for nicotine, 90.6% for alcohol, 97.2% for cannabis and 99.2% for cocaine. Half of the people got out within about 5 years for cocaine and about 26 years for nicotine (Lopez-Quintero, 2011).",
  "s12.h": "Unpredictable rewards hold on harder than predictable ones",
  "s12.step": "11 · The unpredictable",
  "s12.p1": "The neuron in section 04 had a higher firing ramp when the chance of juice was 50%. Maximum uncertainty keeps dopamine active for longer. B. F. Skinner’s pigeons show the effect on behavior. In 1957, Charles Ferster and Skinner compared two schedules. On a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"razaofixa\">fixed-ratio</span> schedule, the pigeon earns food after a fixed number of pecks and pauses after each reward. On a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"razaovar\">variable-ratio</span> schedule, it earns food after the same number of pecks on average, at unpredictable moments, and pecks without stopping. Behavior trained on the variable schedule also takes much longer to die out when the food stops. In the model below, click the button under the two schedules and follow the record of your clicks:",
  "w12a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Predictable reward against unpredictable reward</b><span class=\"wdo\">Click peck in both phases and compare the records. Then see how long you keep going with no reward.</span>",
  "w12a.cap": "Ferster and Skinner (<i>Schedules of Reinforcement</i>, 1957); Fiorillo, Tobler and Schultz (<i>Science</i>, 2003). In 2004, David Zald tested the two schedules with money in people under PET and found a small, diffuse signal.",
  "s12.p2": "<span class=\"mark\">Gambling works entirely within this range of uncertainty</span>, and since 2013 it has been the only behavior that the American psychiatric diagnostic manual, the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dsm\">DSM-5</span>, classifies as an addiction. PET studies show amplified dopamine release in pathological gamblers, with a profile different from that of drugs (Clark, 2019). In Brazil, online betting was regulated in January 2025. According to the National Survey on Alcohol and Drugs (<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"lenad\">LENAD III</span>), from Unifesp, 17.4% of people aged 14 or older bet, 6.8% bet in a risky way, and 0.8%, about 1.4 million people, show a pattern consistent with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tjogo\">gambling disorder</span>:",
  "w12b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">How many Brazilians bet, and how many at risk</b><span class=\"wdo\">See how many bet and, among bettors, the risky share in each income band.</span>",
  "w12b.cap": "LENAD III (Unifesp, 2023 to 2024). Central Bank of Brazil technical note of September 24, 2024, which counts only Pix transfers and does not subtract winnings. Since March 17, 2026, the ECA Digital (Law 15.211/2025) has banned <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"lootbox\">loot boxes</span> in games accessible to children and adolescents.",
  "s12.p3": "Apps use the same mechanics on a smaller scale. Loren Brichter created the pull-to-refresh gesture in 2009 and told <em>The Guardian</em> in 2017 that he had not designed it to be addictive. He now sees it as addictive. No study has measured the dopamine of a person scrolling a feed.",
  "s13.h": "The dopamine detox began as a therapy technique and became a trend",
  "s13.step": "12 · What circulates",
  "s13.p1": "The idea that you can reset your dopamine has a traceable origin. In 2019, the psychologist Cameron Sepah proposed a “dopamine fast” as a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tcc\">cognitive behavioral therapy</span> technique to steer people away from specific impulsive behaviors, and he warned that the name was a figure of speech and that the goal was never to reduce dopamine. <em>The New York Times</em> brought the term to a wide audience in November 2019. During the 2020 lockdowns, YouTube self-help videos turned the fast into a “detox”. In 2023, the “dopamine menu”, a list of activities planned in advance that came from the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tdah\">ADHD</span> community, went viral on TikTok. The searches tell the sequence:",
  "w13a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Dopamine became a trend, and the trend is fading</b><span class=\"wdo\">Scroll to draw the searches, worldwide and in Brazil.</span>",
  "w13a.cap": "Google Trends, queried November 12, 2026, annual averages of the 0 to 100 monthly index. Values are comparable only within each panel. 2026 runs through November 10.",
  "s13.p2": "The cards below carry seven common statements about dopamine. Decide on each one before flipping the card:",
  "w13b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">True or overstated?</b><span class=\"wdo\">Read each statement, choose true or overstated and see the back of the card.</span>",
  "w13b.cap": "The sources for each verdict are on the back of the card and in the sections cited.",
  "s13.p4": "Even so, many people who do a detox report feeling better, and those reports have explanations that do not depend on measuring dopamine. <span class=\"mark\">People on a detox sleep more, spend less time on screens and change their routine,</span> and those effects have been measured: in Castelo’s experiment, two weeks without mobile internet improved mental health. After days without strong stimuli, ordinary things tend to seem more interesting, which fits the surprise signal from section 04 adjusting to the rewards available. That adjustment has been measured in monkeys and never in anyone doing a detox. Add the expectation of improvement, which in Parkinson’s patients released dopamine comparable to a drug, and the fact that many people start at their worst moment, which tends to improve on its own. Those who did well write the report, and those who quit on day three rarely do.",
  "s13.p5": "Nobody has measured the dopamine of someone on a detox because, in living people, it can only be measured with PET, an expensive scan that involves radiation, takes more than an hour and varies about 4% on its own. The researchers who back the idea combine real evidence from other contexts: rats given repeated drugs, scans of addicted people who recover over months, clinical experience with patients and experiments that measure mood and screen time. <span class=\"mark\">The leap is to carry over to phones and sugar, which move the PET signal by 6% to 9%, what was measured with stimulants and cigarettes, which move it by 15% to 37%.</span> In 2008, Deena Weisberg showed that lay readers find an explanation more convincing when it includes a neuroscience term, even an irrelevant one, and “I reset my dopamine” sounds more concrete than “I changed a habit for two weeks”.",
  "s13.p3": "The “dopamine menu” and Sepah’s fast have a useful core once the chemical name is removed. Planning alternatives before boredom arrives and removing the cues for a behavior are behavioral therapy techniques with evidence behind them. Dopamine is in the name as marketing.",
  "s14.h": "What you can reset is what dopamine has learned",
  "s14.step": "13 · What resetting means",
  "s14.p1": "What people call the dopamine system is at least four things, and each is measured in its own way. The first is the fraction of receptors occupied at rest, about a tenth. The second is the number of receptors, stable as a personal trait, which falls with age. The third is the capacity to make dopamine, which changes little. The fourth is the bursts and pauses lasting tenths of a second, which mark surprise. The panel below has a gauge for each. Press the buttons and see which gauge each one moves:",
  "w14a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What actually moves the dopamine system</b><span class=\"wdo\">Press the buttons and see which of the four gauges each one moves.</span>",
  "w14a.cap": "Direction and order of magnitude of each effect, based on the studies cited in sections 08 to 10. The needles are not measurements.",
  "s14.p2": "<span class=\"mark\">The buttons that reliably move the gauges are age, sleep, drugs and medications</span>. No study has measured the dopamine of someone doing a detox. The only gauge that responds to a change in one’s life is the one for bursts and pauses, because it learns. When the cues and rewards of an environment change, the surprise signal from section 04 recalibrates. The burst that fired at the sound of a notification or at breakfast stops firing there, because that cue no longer predicts what it used to. This relearning takes weeks to affect mood and months to change a habit, and it is what the video’s thirty days set in motion.",
  "w14b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Your answers, checked</b><span class=\"wdo\">See the card with what you marked at the start and download the image.</span>",
  "w14b.cap": "The card uses only the answers saved in this browser.",
  "s14.p3": "In the margin of this page, the dopamine neuron keeps marking the background rhythm, four times per second. It fired a burst when the results for coffee, music and the cards differed from what you expected. If you go back to the beginning and redo your guesses, it will fire less, because now you know what is coming.",
  "m.step": "Note on method",
  "m.l1": "The Huberman Lab video appears as the starting point and as one of the voices in section 09. Lembke’s claims were checked against the original studies, and the corrections are in sections 09 and 10.",
  "m.l2": "Almost all the numbers for dopamine measured in humans come from raclopride PET, in samples of 7 to 20 people, with test-retest noise of about 4%. The essay shows the change in the measured signal and avoids converting it into an “increase in dopamine”.",
  "m.l3": "The rat numbers come from <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"microdialise\">microdialysis</span> and were read by hand from the NIDA slides, with a margin of 5% to 10%.",
  "m.l4": "The models in figures 03, 04, 05, 06 and 09 use mechanisms described in the literature, with parameters chosen to fit the screen. They show the shape of each phenomenon. They make no predictions.",
  "m.l5": "The estimate of 4.5 m of axon and up to 2.4 million synapses per human neuron is an extrapolation from rats.",
  "m.l6": "Some numbers come from reliable secondary sources and have not yet been read in the original paper: Hornykiewicz’s one tenth (1960), the placebo percentages in Parkinson’s, the approximate values in Seeman’s graph and Brichter’s remark to <em>The Guardian</em>.",
  "m.l7": "The DOMINION study was funded by the maker of pramipexole. Corvol’s cohort was independent and found a larger effect.",
  "m.l8": "This essay is not medical advice. Stopping daily alcohol, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"benzo\">benzodiazepines</span> or <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"opioides\">opioids</span> abruptly can be dangerous and requires medical supervision.",
  "f.step": "Main sources",
  "f.refs": "\n  <b>Starting point:</b> Huberman Lab Clips, “How to Reset Your Dopamine System &amp; Overcome Addiction”, November 10, 2026. Lembke, <i>Dopamine Nation</i>, 2021 (Brazilian edition, <i>Nação Dopamina</i>, 2022). Google Trends, queried November 12, 2026.<br>\n  <b>History and synthesis:</b> Carlsson, Lindqvist &amp; Magnusson, <i>Nature</i> 1957. Carlsson et al., <i>Science</i> 1958. Ehringer &amp; Hornykiewicz, <i>Klin Wochenschr</i> 1960. Birkmayer &amp; Hornykiewicz, <i>Wien Klin Wochenschr</i> 1961. Cotzias, Van Woert &amp; Schiffer, <i>NEJM</i> 1967.<br>\n  <b>Anatomy and rhythms:</b> Azevedo et al., <i>J Comp Neurol</i> 2009. Pakkenberg et al., 1991. Björklund &amp; Dunnett, <i>Trends Neurosci</i> 2007. Matsuda et al., <i>J Neurosci</i> 2009. Bolam &amp; Pissadaki, <i>Mov Disord</i> 2012. Grace &amp; Bunney, <i>J Neurosci</i> 1984. Liu et al., <i>Cell</i> 2018.<br>\n  <b>Measurement:</b> Di Chiara &amp; Imperato, <i>PNAS</i> 1988. Fiorino, Coury &amp; Phillips, <i>J Neurosci</i> 1997. Bassareo &amp; Di Chiara, <i>Neuroscience</i> 1999. Alakurtti et al., <i>J Cereb Blood Flow Metab</i> 2015.<br>\n  <b>Prediction error and AI:</b> Schultz, Dayan &amp; Montague, <i>Science</i> 1997. Fiorillo, Tobler &amp; Schultz, <i>Science</i> 2003. Rescorla &amp; Wagner, 1972. Sutton, <i>Machine Learning</i> 1988. Dabney et al., <i>Nature</i> 2020. Lowet et al., <i>Nature</i> 2025. ACM, 2024 Turing Award (announced March 2025).<br>\n  <b>Wanting and effort:</b> Berridge &amp; Robinson, <i>Brain Res Rev</i> 1998. Peciña &amp; Berridge, <i>J Neurosci</i> 2005. Salamone et al., <i>Psychopharmacology</i> 1991. Salamone &amp; Correa, <i>Neuron</i> 2012. Treadway et al., <i>J Neurosci</i> 2012. Knutson et al., <i>NeuroReport</i> 2001. Sharot et al., <i>Curr Biol</i> 2009. Kjaer et al., <i>Cogn Brain Res</i> 2002. Manninen et al., <i>J Neurosci</i> 2017. Ferreri et al., <i>PNAS</i> 2019.<br>\n  <b>Dose, psychosis and hormones:</b> Cools &amp; D’Esposito, <i>Biol Psychiatry</i> 2011. Jongkees et al., 2020. Farrell et al., 2012. Seeman et al., <i>Nature</i> 1976. Creese, Burt &amp; Snyder, <i>Science</i> 1976. Kapur et al., <i>Am J Psychiatry</i> 2000. Howes &amp; Kapur, <i>Schizophr Bull</i> 2009.<br>\n  <b>Parkinson’s:</b> Weintraub et al., <i>Arch Neurol</i> 2010. Corvol et al., <i>Neurology</i> 2018. Bernheimer et al., <i>J Neurol Sci</i> 1973. Kordower et al., <i>Brain</i> 2013. Su et al., <i>BMJ</i> 2025. Tabar et al. and Sawamoto et al., <i>Nature</i> 2025.<br>\n  <b>Everyday life:</b> Salimpoor et al., <i>Nat Neurosci</i> 2011. Zald et al., <i>J Neurosci</i> 2004. Koepp et al., <i>Nature</i> 1998. Egerton et al., <i>Neurosci Biobehav Rev</i> 2009. de la Fuente-Fernández et al., <i>Science</i> 2001. Volkow et al., <i>Transl Psychiatry</i> 2015 (caffeine); <i>J Neurosci</i> 2008 and 2012 (sleep). Wang et al., <i>J Nucl Med</i> 2000. Ando et al., <i>J Physiol</i> 2024. Karrer et al., <i>Neurobiol Aging</i> 2017. Eisenhofer et al., <i>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</i> 1997. Šrámek et al., <i>Eur J Appl Physiol</i> 2000. Cohen et al., <i>JAMA Neurology</i> 2022.<br>\n  <b>Tolerance and addiction:</b> Solomon &amp; Corbit, <i>Psychol Rev</i> 1974. Koob &amp; Le Moal, <i>Neuropsychopharmacology</i> 2001. Markou &amp; Koob, 1991. Volkow et al., <i>Life Sci</i> 2000; <i>Am J Psychiatry</i> 2001. Martinez et al., <i>Am J Psychiatry</i> 2009. Nader et al., <i>Nat Neurosci</i> 2006.<br>\n  <b>Time and recovery:</b> Brown &amp; Schuckit, <i>J Stud Alcohol</i> 1988. Hughes, 2007. Budney et al., 2003. Heilig et al., <i>Addict Biol</i> 2010. Thrul et al., 2025. Castelo et al., <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2025. Singh et al., <i>Healthcare</i> 2024. Lally et al., <i>Eur J Soc Psychol</i> 2010. Lopez-Quintero et al., <i>Addiction</i> 2011.<br>\n  <b>The unpredictable:</b> Ferster &amp; Skinner, <i>Schedules of Reinforcement</i>, 1957. Clark, Boileau &amp; Zack, <i>Mol Psychiatry</i> 2019. LENAD III, Unifesp, 2025. Central Bank of Brazil, technical note of September 24, 2024. Law 15.211/2025 (ECA Digital).\n  ",
  "f.foot": "How to reset your dopamine system · interactive essay · data checked against primary sources · November 2026"
};
